/P 1350 0 R
/T (SOAP \(originally "Simple Object Access Protocol", but now just a name\) is a protocol for exchanging structured information between systems. It defines an extensible messaging framework, uses XML technologies, and can run over several underlying protocols \(Gudgin et al., 2007\). A SOAP message has an Envelope that contains an optional Header and a required Body. Errors are reported with a Fault element inside the Body. SOAP services are normally described with a WSDL file. Extensions such as WS- Security add features like message-level signing and encryption.)
/E (SOAP \(originally "Simple Object Access Protocol", but now just a name\) is a protocol for exchanging structured information between systems. It defines an extensible messaging framework, uses XML technologies, and can run over several underlying protocols \(Gudgin et al., 2007\). A SOAP message has an Envelope that contains an optional Header and a required Body. Errors are reported with a Fault element inside the Body. SOAP services are normally described with a WSDL file. Extensions such as WS- Security add features like message-level signing and encryption.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
