/T (Web services communicate through HTTP. In HTTP, a client builds a request message that states what it wants, sends it to a server, and then checks the response to see whether its intention was carried out \(Fielding et al., 2022\). A request contains a method, a URL, headers, and sometimes a body. A response contains a status code, headers, and sometimes a body.)
/E (Web services communicate through HTTP. In HTTP, a client builds a request message that states what it wants, sends it to a server, and then checks the response to see whether its intention was carried out \(Fielding et al., 2022\). A request contains a method, a URL, headers, and sometimes a body. A response contains a status code, headers, and sometimes a body.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
