1724 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 1355 0 R
/T (Purpose: protect data while it travels between client and server. HTTPS is HTTP carried over TLS, and it gives confidentiality, integrity, and server authentication \(Fielding et al., 2022; Rescorla, 2018\). How it works: the client and the server perform a TLS handshake, the server presents a certificate, and both sides create keys to encrypt the traffic. The https URL scheme tells the client to use TLS. Benefits: prevents eavesdropping and tampering on the network, and lets the client check that it is talking to the real server.)
/E (Purpose: protect data while it travels between client and server. HTTPS is HTTP carried over TLS, and it gives confidentiality, integrity, and server authentication \(Fielding et al., 2022; Rescorla, 2018\). How it works: the client and the server perform a TLS handshake, the server presents a certificate, and both sides create keys to encrypt the traffic. The https URL scheme tells the client to use TLS. Benefits: prevents eavesdropping and tampering on the network, and lets the client check that it is talking to the real server.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [35]
>>
endobj
1725 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 1355 0 R
/T (Limitations: it protects data only in transit. It does not protect stored data, does not identify the user, and does not stop attacks that use valid requests. Certificates must be issued and renewed correctly.)
/E (Limitations: it protects data only in transit. It does not protect stored data, does not identify the user, and does not stop attacks that use valid requests. Certificates must be issued and renewed correctly.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [36]
>>
