/T (How it works: there are four roles: the resource owner \(user\), the client \(application\), the authorization server, and the resource server \(API\). The client obtains an access token from the authorization server, then sends it to the API. Tokens can have scopes that limit what they allow. For public clients such as mobile apps and single-page web apps, the Authorization Code flow is used with PKCE \(Sakimura et al., 2015\).)
/E (How it works: there are four roles: the resource owner \(user\), the client \(application\), the authorization server, and the resource server \(API\). The client obtains an access token from the authorization server, then sends it to the API. Tokens can have scopes that limit what they allow. For public clients such as mobile apps and single-page web apps, the Authorization Code flow is used with PKCE \(Sakimura et al., 2015\).)
