2356 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 1373 0 R
/T (Yes, the solution is appropriate for Case 4. The root problem is the duplication of business rules. Moving the rules into one REST API gives a single source of truth, and any change to a rule is made once and becomes visible to both clients. Consistency of validation, calculation, and authorization improves, and the database receives data that was checked in one place. Security is also easier to manage because one boundary can be hardened and monitored.)
/E (Yes, the solution is appropriate for Case 4. The root problem is the duplication of business rules. Moving the rules into one REST API gives a single source of truth, and any change to a rule is made once and becomes visible to both clients. Consistency of validation, calculation, and authorization improves, and the database receives data that was checked in one place. Security is also easier to manage because one boundary can be hardened and monitored.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
2357 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 1373 0 R
/T (The costs are real but manageable. They mainly concern availability, latency, and versioning discipline, and each has a standard mitigation. These costs are lower than the cost of keeping two diverging implementations.)
/E (The costs are real but manageable. They mainly concern availability, latency, and versioning discipline, and each has a standard mitigation. These costs are lower than the cost of keeping two diverging implementations.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
2358 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 1373 0 R
/T (The solution is also proportionate. It uses a single backend with internal layers, not a large set of microservices. For a small or medium system with one team, this keeps the architecture simple. If the system later grows, for example if one function needs to scale separately or a separate team owns it, the backend can be split at that point. Microservices should be a response to a measured need, not the starting assumption.)
/E (The solution is also proportionate. It uses a single backend with internal layers, not a large set of microservices. For a small or medium system with one team, this keeps the architecture simple. If the system later grows, for example if one function needs to scale separately or a separate team owns it, the backend can be split at that point. Microservices should be a response to a measured need, not the starting assumption.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
