2665 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 1380 0 R
/T (Why POST for read-only analysis? The input is a large block of text that may contain job-related content, which does not belong in a URL \(URLs have length limits and can be recorded in logs\). POST allows a request body. These requests have no side effect on stored data, because the service is stateless. Express can therefore retry them safely, with a small retry limit and a timeout \(see B6\).)
/E (Why POST for read-only analysis? The input is a large block of text that may contain job-related content, which does not belong in a URL \(URLs have length limits and can be recorded in logs\). POST allows a request body. These requests have no side effect on stored data, because the service is stateless. Express can therefore retry them safely, with a small retry limit and a timeout \(see B6\).)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
2666 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 1380 0 R
/T (Naming of the endpoints. /extractions is a noun. /analyze and /match are verbs. The course guide says that in REST the verb should come from HTTP, not from the URL \(UTCH BIS, 2026\). The team chose these names because both endpoints perform a stateless calculation and do not create or store a resource.)
/E (Naming of the endpoints. /extractions is a noun. /analyze and /match are verbs. The course guide says that in REST the verb should come from HTTP, not from the URL \(UTCH BIS, 2026\). The team chose these names because both endpoints perform a stateless calculation and do not create or store a resource.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [3]
>>
