6220 0 obj
<<
/Title (Web services communicate through HTTP. In HTTP, a client builds a request message that states what it wants, sends it to a server, and then checks the response to see whether its intention was carried out \(Fielding et al., 2022\). A request contains a method, a URL, headers, and sometimes a body. A response contains a status code, headers, and sometimes a body.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 6206 0 R
/First 6233 0 R
/Last 6233 0 R
/Count -1
>>
endobj
6221 0 obj
<<
/Title (HTTP gives the methods their meaning \(Fielding et al., 2022\). For example, GET reads, and POST creates or processes. GET, PUT, and DELETE are idempotent \(repeating them has the same intended effect as sending them once\), while POST is not guaranteed to be idempotent \(MDN Contributors, n.d.-b\).)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 6095 0 R
/Prev 6210 0 R
/Next 6234 0 R
>>
