/Title (A service contract is a formal, machine-readable description of what a service offers and how to use it. It states which operations exist, what each one accepts, what each one returns, and which errors it can produce. Both the team that builds the service and the teams that use it agree on this document.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 6225 0 R
>>
endobj
6243 0 obj
<<
/Title (The Web Services Description Language \(WSDL\) is an XML language for describing web services \(Chinnici et al., 2007\). A WSDL document describes the data types, the operations and their messages, the protocol binding \(for example, SOAP over HTTP\), and the network address of the service. Tools can read a WSDL file and generate client code automatically.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 6226 0 R
>>
endobj
6244 0 obj
<<
/Title (The OpenAPI Specification \(OAS\) is a standard way to describe HTTP APIs, including REST APIs. Its goal is that people and tools can understand how to call a service without trial and error \(OpenAPI Initiative, 2021\). An OpenAPI document is written in YAML or JSON, and the specification recommends naming the root file openapi.json or openapi.yaml. Version 3.1 uses JSON Schema to describe data \(OpenAPI Initiative, 2021\).)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 6227 0 R
>>
endobj
